              This is the docfile for SFL (Sami's FileLister)

                    SFL is a tool for TransAmiga v1.1x
                      You need Amiga OS release 2 !!
                        call it only from CLI/Shell



                           DISCLAIMER AND RIGHTS

                   The second version of SFL is ready!!!
Why  a  second version? I found that I could throw away some limitations...
I also added some features so that I think there is no other FileLister for
TransAmiga v1.1x that is as powerfull as mine :)

All suggestions and comments are wellcome! Also bug reports if you are able
to fine one :-)))))

Special  greetings  go  to  Tim Aston (auther of TransAmiga)
I wish to thank the following people for testing and ideas:
 Georg 'Gio' Magschok (Sysop of `Gio's Home`)
 Roger Clark (SysOp of `The Hotel California`)

Now  some  stuff  about my rights on the program. I think I have to make it
avalible to all TransAmiga Sysops and I do not ask for a fee if you use it.
I decided to try the concept of Giftware. If you like my tool than write me
a  little  note  via  FidoNet or other ways. If you like my tool such a lot
that you want to send me something else I also would be happy. (For example
one of those new 68040 card for A3000 or a free registration of one of your
programs or what ever you like ;-) ) I like to be taken by surprise :)

You  can  give SFL away to anybody, I just want you to distribute the whole
archive.  If you remove any file you loose the right of using SFL. It is no
problem  for  you  to give away only the original archive, so I really want
you   to   do   that.   Included   in   this   archive   is  a  file  named
ZZZList.foo.displayme.  SysOps should add their BBS here so that people see
the  path  of  the archive. It is also included as XModem transfer likes to
eat bytes at the end of transfered files... So the programs are usable even
if XModem is hungry...

                                 FEATURES

Ok, now we have finished that boring part. Let's have a look on the
features:
- Fast as it's writen in Oberon (the main reason I used that language!)
- can insert a header at the top of the list
- can also insert an endpart (I missed that in all other listers)
- includes only areas with an access level lower or equal the specified
- FLAGS!!! are supported (I deadly missed that in most other listers)
- files considered as new are marked with a * after the date
- can make lists with newfiles only
- sorts the files alphabetically between comments if you like
- in  conjunction with newfiles you can tell SFL to throw away all comments
  that  are  not followed by a file additionally you can say that the first
  comment of the areas should be included anyway.

I think that's it - hope I did not forgot something...

                           INCLUDED EXECUTABLES

There are three executable files included in the distribution archive:
                                    SFL
                                   SFL20
                                   SFL30

SFL  is for use on standard Amiga with MC68000, SFL20 for people who have a
home  for a MC68020 in their Amiga (several turbo cards), SFL30 is compiled
for  those  people  who  own an A3000/3000T or a '30 turbo board there must
also  be  a  68881  or  68882  co-processor.  A  version for MC68040 is not
avalible  as  my comliper has no options for that nice guy... If you have a
MC68020  and  68881/2  or a lonly MC68030 (without 68881/2) I can compile a
version  especially  for  you,  no problem. I did not want to include those
versions as I doubt if many people need them.

SFL  is  heavyly  tested  on an Amiga 3000T and A500 with and without 68020
turbo  card.  There  is no gurantee that it will work on your system but it
should! If you have problems than contact me as discribed below.


                             SYNTAX AND MEMORY

If you try to use SFL with the default stack of 4Kbyte a requester will pop
up  telling  you  that there is no space on stack! The minimum stack to run
SFL  on  my  system is 5060bytes but if you have large fileareas you should
increase  it! I normally use "stack 10000" in my Shell-Startup so I am sure
that there is enough space! A stack of 10000bytes should be possible on any
Amiga  system,  if not you really should think about buying a RAM expension
card (nowadays they're cheep enough).
There  is  one limitation that I should mention now: The lines of textfiles
and  Files.BBS are not allowed to be longer than 98 characters. If they are
longer  the  file  list will be no longer looking as nice as now. Tim Aston
has  added the possebility to include ANSI codes directly in the Files.BBS.
He  srips the codes if a NON-ANSI caller is online. As TransAmiga adds ANSI
codes  when an ANSI-user is online I think this option is just something to
play.  I  did  not  include  stripping  ANSI codes from the Files.BBS so be
carefull. If your lines are not longer than 98 characters you can have them
included. Use one of those freely avalible ANSI stippers if you like to get
rid of the codes in the final list. Perhaps I will add this feature if some
people  tell  me  that  they  can not live without such a feature. The only
negative  thing  is that I will have to write a very nasty and memoy hungry
routine...

Now the syntax:
SFL ?  will  show you the usage. I wanted to keep memory usage at a minimum
so I did not include a check on the size of the Shell. Just make your shell
window  bigger if you miss some lines of description... Try it and you will
see what I mean. The commandline syntax is:

SFL [outname] [-C<path>] [-H<name>] [-E<name>] [-A<name>] [-Dx] [-Lx] [-Ff]
[-N] [-S] [-X] [-1] [-Z]

I  thought  about  making a config file as there are a lot parameters but I
don't like to have a configfile for every tool I use - it's enough! I think
I  chose  good  defaults,  so  you  should  not need all parameters. As all
parameters  are optional SFL will try to create a filelist if you just type
SFL to the Shell.
Now, let's explain the parameters:

outname:
  This  is  the  name  and  path  of the file that will be created and that
  contains  the  filelist.  If  you  don't  specify  a  name  SFL will take
  T:FileList  as  default  and  add  a .lst for a normal list or .new for a
  newfiles  list.  ATTENTION: If you specify a name SFL will add .lst for a
  normal  listing  or .new for a newfiles listing. If your filepath or name
  allready contents a dot (.) nothing is added!

-C<path>:
  This is the config path of TransAmiga where all TransAmiga's config files
  are  stored. Normally you should not need to specify this. This option is
  only  needed  if you did not use the defaults of TransCfg while insalling
  TransAmiga.  The  default  is "BBS:Configs". If you used an other path in
  TransCfg you have to specify it here!

-H<name>:
  This is the path and name of a standard ASCII text file that you want to
  be  added  at  the top of the filelist. If you don't specify this option
  nothing  will  be inserted. The text file has to be in Amiga format, SFL
  adds  the  carriage return that is needed by IBM compatibles to read the
  textfile.  You  can not say SFL to leave out that CR's. If you really do
  not want them you can gat a little program from me that strips them. But
  if  the  possebility  exists  that an user with IBM compatible equipment
  likes  to  read  your  filelist  leave the CR's in. Your Amiga users can
  easyly strip them with most editors by replacing CTRL-M with nothing or
  like CygnusEd with the option "Strip CR block".

-E<name>:
  This is the path and name of a standard ASCII text file that you want to
  be added at the end of the filelist. This works in the same way as the -H
  option so the same limitations are made...

-A<name>:
  This is the path and name of a special text file. If this option is given
  and  -Z  is  NOT  used the spcified file will be taken as pattern for the
  area  summaries.  An  example  file  is  included  called "areasumm". Two
  special  codes are replaced by SFL: CTRL-A will be replaced by a 6 digits
  long number (with leading zeros), the number of files in the area. CTRL-B
  will  be  replaced  by  a 10 digits long number (also leading zeros), the
  total  bytes  the  files  of  the  area have. The size of the file is not
  limited  and you can  specify the to CTRL-codes as often as you want, BUT
  BE CAREFULL! The CTRL-character is removed and the digits inserted so you
  will have 5 (CTRL-A) or 9 (CTRL-B) characters more in that line! Examine
  the given example and you will see how it works. If this option is not
  used the internal default will be used (they are German !).

-Dx:
  This  option  allows  you  to  specify  the  age of newfiles in days. The
  default is 7 days. I don't think you will specify more than 999 days here
  but  if  you  like  to you can. Replace the x by the number you like. The
  number will be taken to find out if a file should be considered as new or
  not.  If  a  file  is detected as a new file an asterix (*) will be added
  after  the  date  of  the file. This option is also used for the newfiles
  list.  The  number you specify here tells SFL the age of files to include
  in  the  listing  (only  in  conjunction  with -N option). This option is
  heavily  dependend  on  the  date  of  your  System, so you should have a
  working  battery  backedup clock or set the date to the actual one before
  using SFL.

-Lx:
  This  option  allows you to specify the access level that should be taken
  as   maximum.   Replace  the  x by the level you like. The default is 50.
  Possible   numbers   are  0 to 255 as this are the access levels that are
  allowed  by  TransAmiga. If you specify 255 you will not include all file
  areas  if  you gave them flags. If you want to include all files you will
  also need the -F option. Let's say you want a filelist for you users with
  access  level 100. You specify -L100 and all areas that have level 100 or
  lower  are included if they match the flags(!). If this is not clear some
  experiments will help :) 

-Ff:
  This option allows you to specify the access flags. TransAmiga allows you
  to  have  a  set  of eight flags to increase control on users access. The
  flags  are  only  a  second  way of control, tha major way are the access
  levels.  If  you  do not use this option only areas with no flags will be
  included, that's the default (-F--------). A flag is set if a X is put at
  the  position of the flag, the same way as TransAmiga asks for them. Now,
  let's  make an exampe. You specified -L100 and -F-X---X--. All areas that
  have a level of 100 or lower will be included if they match the following
  flagcombinations:  --------,  -X------,  -----X-- and -X---X--. All other
  areas will not be included. So don't forget this option!

-N:
  This  is  a  simple swich. It swiches on the newfiles. All files that are
  considered as new are included. If this option is NOT specified ALL files
  will  be  included.  Use the -Dx option to tell SFL what age of files you
  like  to  be taken as new. Newfiles are always marked with an asterix (*)
  after their date, that does not depend on this option.

-S:
  This  is  also  a  swich.  It  swiches  on the alphabetical sort of files
  between  two  comments.  If your Files.BBS does not contain a comment the
  whole area will be sorted. This option works also with newfiles.

-X:
  An  other  switch.  It  swiches  on the deletion of commands that are not
  followed  by files. This option has ony an effect on newfile listings. If
  you  have  lots of comments in your Files.BBS this option throws away all
  comments  that  are not followed by a file (newfile in this case) that is
  written  to  the  outputfile.  This option hels saving space and transfer
  time for newfile listings :) Try it and you will see what I mean.

-1:
  The  next  switch...  It  tells  SFL  to  include  the  startcomment of a
  Files.BBS  in  any  case.  This  option  has  only  an  effect if used in
  conjunction  with the -X switch in all other cases it is done anyways. If
  you  have  a comment at the beginning of your Files.BBS you may like this
  one to be included although you want to get rid of all the other comments
  that  are  not  followed by a newfile. I put a general description of the
  purpose of the area at the first lines of every Files.BBS and I like this
  one to be included in the newfile lists in any case, if followed by a new
  file  or  not. So I use this option. This only works if the first comment
  is  the  first  thing  of  the Files.BBS, if there is a file at the first
  line  of  the  Files.BBS this option will have no effect. Again, try this
  option and you will see what it is for...

-Z:
  This  is  the last swich I have to explain :) It disables the summarys at
  the  end  of  the  areas.  If  not  specified the number of files in this
  area  and  their  total bytes are written at the end of each area. Try it
  both possebilities and than decide wich you like more...


